---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ForestPlot from '../components/ForestPlot.astro';
import data from '../data/trust-in-medical-ai/trust-by-domain.json';
import { href } from '../utils/url';
---

<Base description="Behavioral scientist studying how people decide whether to trust AI. Interactive evidence from my own research.">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="hero">
      <h1>People trust AI less than doctors, even when the advice is identical.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        I'm Matthew Leitao, a behavioral scientist. I design experiments on how people decide whether to trust AI,
        analyze the data in R, and report what the numbers support, including the null results.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="section gap" aria-labelledby="gap-title">
      <h2 id="gap-title">The gap shows up in every kind of medical decision</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        In Study 2 of my dissertation, 106 adults rated how much they trusted a doctor and an AI making the same
        medical judgment. Each dot is the average difference, and the line is its 95% confidence interval. Hover over
        a row or tab to it for the exact statistics.
      </p>
      <div class="gap-grid">
      <ForestPlot
        rows={data.rows}
        xMin={-1}
        xMax={5}
        xLabel="Doctor minus AI, in trust rating points"
        leftLabel="AI trusted more"
        rightLabel="Doctor trusted more"
        animate
      />
      <div>
      <p class="takeaway">
        The gap is large in all five domains, with effect sizes from d = 1.24 to 1.33. It barely moves with the type
        of decision, so the cause is more likely in how people see the decision-maker than in the medical content.
        The rest of the dissertation tests that idea.
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">
        Source: Leitao (2024), Georgetown University dissertation, Study 2, Table 7.
        <a href={href('/projects/trust-in-medical-ai/')}>Read the full case study</a>.
      </p>
      </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="do-title">
      <h2 id="do-title">What I do</h2>
      <ul class="rows do">
        <li>
          <h3>Design experiments</h3>
          <p>
            Preregistered, randomized studies, from the first hypothesis through recruitment, analysis and write-up.
            My dissertation ran six of them and recruited more than 3,000 participants.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Model messy human data</h3>
          <p>
            Multilevel, longitudinal and causal models in R, applied to surveys, daily diaries and clinical-trial data.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Lead research teams</h3>
          <p>
            Managed five research assistants across six or seven concurrent projects at Georgetown. Now manage six
            projects at the National Institutes of Health.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="next">
        See all the <a href={href('/projects/')}>projects</a>, or check the
        <a href={href('/skills/')}>evidence behind each skill</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero {
    padding-top: clamp(40px, 8vw, 96px);
  }

  .hero h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 6.4vw, 4.6rem);
    line-height: 1.02;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    max-width: 20ch;
  }

  .hero .lede {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .gap-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2rem 4rem;
    align-items: start;
  }

  @media (min-width: 1000px) {
    .gap-grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 480px) minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }

  .sub {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 1rem;
    margin: 0.75rem 0 1.75rem;
  }

  .takeaway {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .do > li {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.35rem 2rem;
  }

  .do p {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 58ch;
  }

  @media (min-width: 760px) {
    .do > li {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 2fr);
    }
  }

  .next {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
</style>
